00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.050
There's an algorithm called Momentum or Gradient Descent with Momentum that almost always works

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.690
faster than the standard gradient descent algorithm.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.290
In one sentence, the basic idea is to compute an exponentially weighted average of your

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:18.890
gradients and then to use that gradient to update your weights instead.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:23.530
In this video, let's unpack that one sentence description and see how you can actually implement

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:24.530
this.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:28.730
As the most of an example, let's say that you're trying to optimize a cost function

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.650
which has contours like this.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.550
So the red dot denotes the position of the minimum.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:40.730
Maybe you start a gradient descent here and if you take one iteration of gradient descent,

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.730
either batch or mini batch gradient descent, maybe you end up heading there.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:48.930
But now you're on the other side of this ellipse, you kind of, and if you take another step

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.890
of gradient descent, maybe you end up doing that.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.730
And then another step, another step, and so on.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:00.930
And you see that gradient descents will sort of take a lot of steps, right?

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:07.370
Just slowly oscillate toward the minimum.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:12.730
And these up and down oscillations slows down gradient descent and prevents you from using

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:14.690
a much larger learning rate.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:19.370
In particular, if you were to use a much larger learning rate, you might end up overshooting

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.810
and end up diverging like so.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.770
And so the need to prevent the oscillations from getting too big forces you to use a learning

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.910
rate that's not itself too large.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:35.090
Another way of viewing this problem is that on the vertical axis, you want your learning

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.090
to be a bit slower because you don't want those oscillations.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:46.190
But on the horizontal axis, you want faster learning, right?

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:50.250
Because you want it to aggressively move from left to right toward that minimum, toward

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.050
that red dot.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:58.870
So here's what you can do if you implement gradient descents with momentum.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:08.910
On each iteration, or more specifically, during iteration t, you would compute the usual

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:16.190
derivatives, dW, dB, I'll omit the super, super square bracket L's, but you compute

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.310
dW, dB on the current mini batch.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:23.030
And if you're using batch gradient descent, then, you know, the current mini batch would

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:24.310
be just your whole batch.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.790
And this works as well for batch gradient descent.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.630
So if your current mini batch is your entire training set, this works fine as well.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:46.030
And then what you do is you compute V, dW, to be beta VdW plus 1 minus beta dW.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:53.670
So this is similar to when we were previously computing V theta equals beta V theta plus

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.990
1 minus beta theta t.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:02.750
So it's computing a moving average of the derivatives for W you're getting.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:14.100
And then you similarly compute V, dB equals that plus 1 minus beta times dB.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:20.940
And then you would update your weights using W, gets updated as W minus the learning rate

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:26.860
times, instead of updating it with dW with the derivative, you would update it with V,

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.300
dW.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:35.700
And similarly, B gets updated as B minus alpha times V, dB.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:41.300
So what this does is smooth out the steps of gradient descent.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:46.860
For example, let's say that in the last few derivatives you computed were this, this, this,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.420
this, this.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.940
If you average out these gradients, you find that the oscillations in the vertical direction

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.760
will tend to average out to something closer to zero.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:01.780
So in the vertical direction, where you want to slow things down, this will average out

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.180
positive and negative numbers.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.580
So the average will be closer to zero.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.900
Whereas on the horizontal direction, all the derivatives are pointing to the right in

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.100
the horizontal direction.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.420
So the average in the horizontal direction will still be pretty big.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:19.700
So that's why with this algorithm, with a few iterations, you find that the gradient descent

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:26.320
with momentum ends up eventually just taking steps that are much smaller oscillations

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.580
in the vertical direction, but are more directed to what the horizontal, to just moving

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.780
quickly in the horizontal direction.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.660
And so this allows your algorithm to, you know, take a more straightforward path or

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:43.060
less to damp out the oscillations in its path to the minimum.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:49.180
One intuition for this momentum, which works for some people and not for everyone, is that

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.580
if you're trying to minimize, you know, a bow shape function, right, this, this is

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:58.020
really the contours of a bow, um, I guess I'm not very good at drawing, but you're

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.380
trying to minimize this type of bow shape function, then these derivative terms, you

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:11.660
can think of as providing acceleration to a ball that you're rolling downhill.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:21.220
And these momentum terms you can think of as representing the velocity.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:27.100
And so imagine that you have a bow and you take a ball and the derivative imparts

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.940
acceleration to this little ball.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:32.540
It's a little ball that's rolling down this hill, right?

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:38.860
And so it rolls faster and faster because of acceleration and beta, because this

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.620
number a little bit less than one, this plays a role of friction and it prevents

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.500
your ball from, you know, speeding up without limit.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:52.420
But so rather than gradient descent, just taking every single step independently

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:57.420
of all previous steps, now your little ball can roll downhill and gain momentum,

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:01.460
but you can accelerate down this bow and therefore gain momentum.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:05.780
I find that this ball rolling down a bow analogy, it seems to work for some

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.980
people who enjoy physics intuitions, but it doesn't work for everyone.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:13.940
So if this analogy of a ball rolling down a bow doesn't work for you, don't

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:14.900
worry about it.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.140
Finally, let's look at some details on how you implement this.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:19.220
Here's the algorithm.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:26.020
And so you now have two hyper parameters, the learning rate alpha, as well as this

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.460
parameter beta, which controls your exponentially weighted average.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:33.100
The most common value for beta is 0.9.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.620
We're averaging over the last 10 days temperature.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.780
So this is like averaging over the last 10 iterations gradients.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:43.060
And in practice, beta equals 0.9 works very well.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.980
Feel free to try different values and do some hyper parameter search, but 0.9

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.020
appears to be a pretty robust value.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.020
Well, and how about bias correction, right?

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:58.060
So do you want to take VdW and Vdb and divide it by 1 minus beta to the T?

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:03.100
In practice, people don't usually do this because after just 10 iterations, your

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.420
moving average will have warmed up and there's no longer a bias estimate.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:11.820
So in practice, I don't really see people bothering with bias correction

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.780
when implementing gradient descent or momentum.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:18.900
And of course, this process initializes with VdW equals 0.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.260
Note that this is a matrix of zeros with the same dimension as dW, which is

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:30.620
the same dimension as W. And Vdb is also initialized to a vector of 0.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:35.300
So the same dimension as dB, which in turn has the same dimension as B.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:41.740
Finally, I just mentioned that if you read the literature on gradient descent with momentum,

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:48.580
often you see it with this term omitted, with this 1 minus beta term omitted.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:57.020
So you end up with VdW equals beta, VdW plus dW.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:02.780
And the net effect of using this version in purple is that VdW ends up being scaled

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:07.340
by a factor of 1 minus beta, or really 1 over 1 minus beta.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:11.780
And so when you're performing these gradient descent updates, alpha just needs to change

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:16.180
by corresponding value of, or 1 over 1 minus beta.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:18.740
In practice, both of these will work just fine.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:23.700
It just affects what's the best value of the learning rate alpha.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:30.100
But I find that this particular formulation is a little less intuitive, because one impact

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:34.260
of this is that if you end up tuning the hyperparameter beta, then this affects

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.740
the scaling of VdW and Vdb as well.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.660
And so you end up needing to retune the learning rate alpha as well, maybe.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:47.380
So I personally prefer the formulation that I had written here on the left, rather than

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.500
leaving out the 1 minus beta term.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:54.940
So I tend to use the formula on the left, the printed formula, with the 1 minus beta term.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:09:00.180
But both versions, having beta equals 0.9 is a common choice of hyperparameter.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.300
It's just that alpha, the learning rate, will need to be tuned differently

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:04.780
for these two different versions.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:07.460
So that's it for gradient descent with momentum.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:12.140
This will almost always work better than the straightforward gradient descent algorithm

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:13.140
without momentum.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.100
But there's still other things we could do to speed up your learning algorithm.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.980
Let's continue talking about these in the next couple of videos.
